Quick Review:
This is an old (2009-ish) korean game (for those who play korean games, you know the grinding is real) , with micro-transactions everywhere. The game is really fun at the beginning, until you realise how much effort you'll have to put to get the most basic digimon, which will get slaughtered by mobs at the same level as it. If you're really craving a digimon online game, I'd recommend DigimonLinkz at the appstore nextdoor.
Long Review:
The game is free to play, but to have any chance at anything, you need to put in some cash since:
- Inventory is limited (items and digimons);
- Evolution requires cash shop items;

About the cash shop:
- Items seem cheaper now than they were before;
- The cosmetic items and auras have a time limit;
- LOOT BOXES (the eggs you buy have to be scanned, and you can get lvl 3 ( weaker than mobs same level ), lvl 4 and lvl 5 (way stronger than mobs same level);
- The economy is more f*cked-up than Venezuela's, so you'll most likely resort to the cash shop
About digimons:
- Scanning and enhancing uses consumable items, and may fail, so you need to buy backups (so you buy an egg, the chips, and a backup). Failed attempts may destroy the item;
- As said above, you need certain items to unlock evolutions and riding mode, all from the cash shop;
- They have a good variety of digimons and digivolution lines.
About gameplay and story:
- Point and click game (they added WASD now with...different degrees of success);
- Press F3 until you or your digimon dies;
- Game has a stamina mechanic, to waste even more of your time;
- Game has raids and dungeons, as most MMOs have;
- Story more convoluted than Final Fantasy games;
Random thoughts:
- Devs don't seem to be too bothered about toxic players or botters;
- They changed the initial maps, killing a bit of the nostalgia I had;
- Not as laggy as it was last year;
- Bots everywhere;
- Can't merge with your old account, so bye-bye money you invested;
- Devs are really trying to get new and old players in, so I wonder how longer it'll last;
- No pvp (thank god, the korean one is awful);
- ...I miss the game's 2009 launch...
Please don't hate me. Or do, I don't care.

### UPDATE. IMPORTANT EDIT: I'm downrating this one due to endless bugs that cripple the gameplay. When you spend 1 hour trying to figure out how to end a mission that has been haunted by a severe bug, it means you are not playing the game any more. I am seriously tired of how annoying this game is. Intriguing? Yes. Playable? Nah.
So here I am, defending an airport. Except there is this guy leading the team who keeps setting waypoints some 798 m away from where action is... possibly following an enemy that the system respawned where it shouldn't have. Every AI is running around like an idiot trying to make sense of a map that is simply too big for the mission at end (in this mission, you use only 10% of the entire map for meaningful purposes. The rest is but a barren wasteland where occasional respawns make it impossible to complete the mission).
But that's not all! So here I am. I climbed the ladder and I am facing a sniper. Yeah, a sniper wearing a headscarf. I look at him. He looks at me. Why isn't he shooting me? I mean, I'm even playing Veteran here, but this guy does not seem willing to end my life. He simply stares at me! That's nothing short of incredible.
...And why am I staring at him? Suprise: I ran out of bullets and have just realized this game has no control to hit him in the face. Man, I cannot even push you down the building, hoping the fall would kill you. It's a stall.
Of course I can go back and fetch some ammo, but the sheer idea of facing this scripted thing that stares back at me and doesn't shoot... paired with my inability to fight back with my bare hands... I mean, not even COD touched the bottom of the barrel. PMC did!
### OLD REVIEW FOLLOWS ###
ArmA 2: PMC builds on Operation Arrowhead, but from a different viewpoint -- i.e. that of private contractors who work in war zones.
Some interesting things:
- The plot is intriguing;
- Scenarios are a pleasure for the eye;
- Somewhat challenging;
- It crashes way less than all the other arma games;
- Easier controls.
However, be warned that it is not your traditional ArmA 2 game, because it downgrades a lot of aspects that made ArmA 2 famous. I do not take an issue with that: it is a matter of choices, but just do expect something a bit more like COD and a bit less like ArmA.
Some things to consider:
- Differently form other ArmA games, here you spend most of your time on the field as a single individual. Most of this game takes away all the "troop management thing" that was typical of ArmA;
- The world is not open-ended. Scenarios are broad and fully explorable, yet they contain very little beyond the mission's limited scope. I remember running around, searching houses... but, in the end, that's just a tour of the gaming world, but it does not add to its background and story; at best, you'll end up in a nicely designed empty shelled building;
- Most mission and plots are mono-rail: you have to do A, B, C, and D in line to get to the end. It feels like a rollercoaster most of the time. Nothing against this, yet, again, this is not the open-ended world but rather a glorified version of the good old Call of Duty series. Just stay on track and do what you are told;
- Some other reviewers noticed that sometimes realism is sacrificed to the sake of the storyline. I think the issue is a bit deeper than that: there is a mission in which you have to take down a series of vehicles one by one with an anti-materiel rifle. It is not that the mission isn't challenging per se... however, with some grain of salt you will keep asking yourself: don't the people on the vehicles communicate with each other? Do they keep standing there or following the same scripted path just for you to drop in and take a shot? Basically, at some stage this game will make you feel like the AI could have done better;
- Game-breaking situations, in which a mission is extremely difficult if you play as the gunner, but it gets piece of cake if you set yourself on the driver's seat...
- Longevity is not very good: the time you see next to my name (28 hrs) is what it takes for you to get to the mission before the last one, assuming you will go through some missions more than once to maximize your results. This means that the entire campaign is much shorter if you consider the actual game time required to finish the game.
...and so on.
Final assessment: game is recommended if you want a different kind of experience with the ArmA engine. Possibly a more affordable and easy one... and if you are intrigued by Middle-Eastern scenarios. But that's about it.

